---
// src/pages/blog/shapewear-qc-and-durability-testing.astro
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../../components/Picture.astro';
import BlogSchema from '../../components/BlogSchema.astro';
import { blogSchemaBySlug } from '../../data/blog-schema';

const entry = blogSchemaBySlug['shapewear-qc-and-durability-testing'];
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Shapewear QC: What to Test Before Bulk Ships"
  description="The QC protocol we run on compression garments - recovery, seam bond strength, burst, and wash cycles - with pass criteria and sampling plans."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Shapewear QC</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark mb-8">
      <Picture src="blog-shapewear-detail.webp" alt="Compression garment panel being measured and inspected on a cutting table at the Charlizi factory" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" priority sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
    </div>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">December 10, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">Shapewear QC: What to Test Before Bulk Ships</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        Shapewear either works on the third wash or it does not work at all. Everything
        before that point - the visual check, the measurement, the hand-feel in the
        inspection room - can pass on a garment that is three weeks away from going slack.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the test protocol we run before a compression order leaves the building,
        with the numbers we consider pass, and the sampling plan attached to each test.
        It is deliberately short. Ten tests, not forty, and each one tied to a field
        complaint it is designed to prevent.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Fabric weight and stretch, on the roll not the garment</h2>
      <p>
        Test incoming fabric before cutting, not the finished piece. Once a bodysuit is
        cut and bonded, a faulty roll has already become a rejection.
      </p>
      <p>Four samples per roll. Tolerance ±5%.</p>
      <p>
        Two more numbers worth recording on the intake sheet.
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Weight:</strong> grams per square metre measured on a GSM cutter, four samples per roll, tolerance ±5%. A roll 8% light will feel fine and compress badly.</li>
        <li><strong>Stretch and recovery:</strong> a strip cycled to a fixed extension, typically 80% of the garment's designed stretch, then released. Recovery should be 92% or better. Powernet below 90% goes slack in the first weeks of wear, not at end of life.</li>
        <li><strong>Elongation direction:</strong> check separately lengthwise and crosswise. Many shapewear failures are a fabric where the high-stretch direction ended up running around the body instead of down it, and that is a cutting problem the fabric test cannot catch but the marker review will.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Fabric testing is the cheapest place in the whole process to find a problem. Every
        yard rejected here saves a completed garment later.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Compression on the finished garment</h2>
      <p>
        The headline number, and the one most factories cannot show. Compression is
        measured with a calibrated band tester at defined body positions - thigh, hip,
        waist, under-bust - on a specified mannequin size, at a specified point in the
        wear cycle.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Position matters more than the number.</strong> A single "compression: 15-20 mmHg" figure is a marketing number. Zoned compression has to be checked per zone.</li>
        <li><strong>Check at wear 0 and after 30 cycles.</strong> The loss of compression over the first month of use is the thing the customer actually experiences.</li>
        <li><strong>Pass criterion:</strong> no zone falls below the lower bound of its design band after 30 cycles. See the <a href="/blog/shapewear-manufacturing">shapewear manufacturing guide</a> for how the zones are set at development.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Seam bond strength</h2>
      <p>
        Bonded seams are the highest-risk construction in shapewear. A sewn seam fails
        loudly and immediately; a bonded seam fails quietly, at a load the customer
        reaches naturally when she pulls the garment on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Peel test on a cut strip: 180° peel at a defined rate, recorded in newtons per
        25 mm width. We specify a floor of 6 N/25 mm for bonded edges and 8 N/25 mm on
        high-tension seams such as the under-bust and the centre front. Below those,
        edge lift is a matter of when.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sample plan: five strips from each seam type per inspection lot, and always from
        the seam with the highest tension in the panel - usually not the one the buyer
        names.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Seam elongation</h2>
      <p>
        The companion test to peel strength, and the one that catches a seam that holds
        under a straight pull and fails when the garment stretches. Bond the seam, then
        extend the joined strip to garment stretch.
      </p>
      <p>
        A seam that delaminates at 40% extension will survive the inspection room and
        fail every time someone sits down. We extend to 80% and require the bond to hold
        through ten cycles.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Burst strength</h2>
      <p>
        A diaphragm burst test applies pressure to a clamped fabric or panel until it
        ruptures. It answers the question peel and stretch tests cannot: is there one
        weak point in this panel that will be the first thing to let go?
      </p>
      <p>
        Reported in kPa. The figure that matters is the minimum, not the mean. One sample
        at 60% of the batch median means a problem in the fabric or the bond, and the
        whole lot needs a wider sample before shipping.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Wash durability</h2>
      <p>
        The test that links everything else to the customer's actual experience. There is
        no single standard, so specify one: we run 30 cycles at 40 °C, gentle cycle, line
        dry, then repeat measurements 1 to 5.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Dimensional stability: within 5% of the original measurements.</li>
        <li>Compression: within 10% of the wear-0 zone values.</li>
        <li>Seam bond: no delamination on any tested seam.</li>
        <li>Visual: no pilling above grade 4, no colour change above grade 4-5 on the grey scale.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Thirty cycles is roughly three months of twice-weekly wear. Most complaints about
        shapewear land between month one and month four, which is why the cycle count is
        not negotiable downwards for cost reasons.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Colourfastness - washing and crotch contact</h2>
      <p>
        Two different tests that get confused. Wash fastness is about the garment not
        bleeding onto itself or a light load. Perspiration and rubbing fastness is about
        the garment not discolouring where it contacts skin.
      </p>
      <p>
        On shapewear the second one matters more than buyers expect. A black bodysuit
        that is fine after washing may grey out at the crotch seam and the under-band
        within a fortnight. Test with an alkaline perspiration solution, and specify the
        grade rather than accepting "passed".
      </p>

      <h2>8. Zipper, hook and hardware strength</h2>
      <p>
        Anything metal gets a pull test and a cycle test. Hook-and-eye tape: 500 open and
        close cycles with a load applied, then visual inspection of the tape. Zips: slider
        pull strength and 300 cycles through the full run of the zip.
      </p>
      <p>
        The failure to look for is not the hook breaking - it is the tape tearing away
        from the garment edge, which takes the whole closure with it.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Construction symmetry</h2>
      <p>
        Not a lab test. A measurement test on the finished garment: left and right side
        seam lengths, panel widths, and compression zone boundaries should not differ by
        more than 3 mm.
      </p>
      <p>
        Asymmetry is the defect customers notice first, before any of the durability
        numbers, and it comes from cutting or from the bonding fixture rather than the
        fabric. It costs almost nothing to check and catches whole lots of misaligned
        panels.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Sampling plan and what AQL you are actually buying</h2>
      <p>
        Every test above means nothing without a sampling plan. A 100% visual check of a
        cosmetic defect and a five-strip peel test on a 5,000-piece lot are not the same
        level of assurance, and buyers often assume they bought the second when they
        bought the first.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Visual and measurement defect:</strong> AQL 2.5 general inspection level II, the usual apparel standard.</li>
        <li><strong>Functional defects</strong> (seam delamination, closure failure): AQL 1.0 or tighter, because a functional failure is a return, not a discount.</li>
        <li><strong>Destructive lab tests:</strong> per inspection lot, not per order, and never waived to save time on a reorder.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write the sampling plan into the purchase order with the test list attached. A
        factory that quotes an AQL it cannot support will pass inspection on the paperwork
        and fail in the field.
      </p>

      <h2>The pattern behind most shapewear complaints</h2>
      <p>
        Nearly all of the failures above share one property: they are invisible at
        incoming inspection and obvious at week four. That is the whole argument for a
        documented durability protocol - not because buyers love testing, but because the
        alternative is discovering the problem in the review section.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the garment sits next to a matching bra or panty in a set, the set has its own
        consistency requirements - see <a href="/blog/bra-set-development">bra set
        development</a>. If you are still choosing a supplier, the
        <a href="/blog/choosing-bra-manufacturer">manufacturer selection guide</a> covers
        what a factory should be able to show you before you place the order.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Shipping shapewear and want a durability protocol attached?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone mt-1">We test compression per zone, bond strength at 6–8 N/25 mm, and 30 wash cycles before a lot ships, with the sampling plan written into the PO. MOQ 200–300 pcs per design and colourway. <a href="/contact" class="text-accent hover:underline">Ask for our QC checklist</a> or see the <a href="/products/shapewear" class="text-accent hover:underline">shapewear range</a>.</p>
    </div>
  <BlogSchema slug="shapewear-qc-and-durability-testing" entry={entry} />

  </article>
</BlogLayout>
